# PHASE 1 — AUDIT AND ARCHITECTURE

## 1. Inspect the Entire Existing Application

Before writing code, inspect:

- All Kotlin source files.
- All Compose screens and reusable UI components.
- Navigation graph and destination definitions.
- Bottom navigation dock.
- Buttons, icon buttons, cards, menus, dialogs, and sheets.
- Word editor and formatting toolbar.
- Spreadsheet grid, formula bar, and sheet tabs.
- Presentation editor and slide filmstrip.
- PDF reader and annotation tools.
- Search and filtering interfaces.
- Profile and Settings.
- Save, loading, success, and error states.
- Existing gesture handlers.
- Existing haptic or vibration implementations.
- Current Compose, Material, Navigation, and AndroidX dependency versions.
- Current minimum SDK and supported Android versions.

Create an inventory of interactive components and their current behavior.

Identify duplicated click handlers, conflicting gesture detectors, animations triggered by recomposition, and UI elements that currently provide no feedback.

Do not assume that a component is implemented just because a corresponding screen exists.

## 2. Build a Reusable Motion Architecture

Create a centralized motion system instead of scattering arbitrary animation constants across the application.

Use a structure appropriate to the existing project, such as:

- `MotionTokens.kt`
- `MotionSpecs.kt`
- `MotionModifiers.kt`
- `HapticFeedbackManager.kt`
- `HapticType.kt`
- `MotionPreferences.kt`

These names are suggestions, not mandatory filenames. Follow the repository's existing architecture.

Centralize:

- Animation durations.
- Easing curves.
- Spring stiffness and damping.
- Enter and exit transitions.
- Press-response parameters.
- Selection-indicator transitions.
- Gesture thresholds and settling behavior.
- Haptic semantic mappings.
- Reduced-motion behavior.

Prefer strongly typed, reusable definitions rather than unexplained numeric literals throughout the codebase.

Keep animation state separate from document content and business logic.

## 3. Suggested Motion Timing System

Use the following values as starting points. Tune them through actual emulator testing instead of treating them as inflexible requirements.

| Interaction | Target duration |
|---|---:|
| Immediate press response | 60–100 ms |
| Icon state change | 100–160 ms |
| Small selection transition | 120–180 ms |
| Tooltip or compact popup | 120–190 ms |
| Dropdown or contextual menu | 150–220 ms |
| Bottom sheet entrance | 220–320 ms |
| Dialog entrance | 170–240 ms |
| Small content transition | 160–240 ms |
| Full-screen navigation | 200–300 ms |
| Dock selection movement | 180–280 ms |
| Success-state transition | 150–250 ms |

These are design targets, not delays to insert before an action. Business operations, saving, and navigation must not be artificially delayed until animations finish.

Use suitable easing curves:

- Quick ease-out for press feedback and menu entrances.
- Smooth ease-in-out for reversible visual state changes.
- Spring-based movement for the dock indicator and interactive drag settling.
- A short linear or gently eased transition for progress indicators where appropriate.

Avoid a spring animation for every component. Not all UI movement needs a physical bounce.

