# PHASE 3 — LIQUID GLASS MOTION

## 7. Bottom Dock Selection Animation

Enhance the existing Liquid Glass bottom dock without replacing its design.

When the user switches destinations:

- Move the selected indicator smoothly toward the new item.
- Use a restrained spring animation with minimal overshoot.
- Transition selected icon tint and label visibility.
- Keep unselected items stable.
- Avoid shifting the dock's outer dimensions unnecessarily.
- Ensure the indicator always matches the actual navigation destination.

The indicator must not lag behind navigation or continue animating toward an outdated destination after rapid taps.

If the user quickly taps Home, Files, Settings, and Profile, navigation and selected-state updates must remain deterministic.

## 8. Glass Surface Interaction

For interactive glass surfaces, use subtle changes in:

- Surface tint.
- Highlight opacity.
- Shadow elevation.
- Border highlight.
- Selected-state emphasis.

Keep these effects local to the interacted component.

Do not continuously animate blur radius, surface reflections, or full-screen gradients. Continuous blur animation is expensive and rarely adds useful feedback.

Do not make the dock pulse constantly.

## 9. Modal and Overlay Material Transitions

For existing menus, dialogs, popovers, and bottom sheets:

- Animate opacity and modest vertical movement.
- Use a restrained scale transition only where it suits the component.
- Keep the background scrim transition synchronized with the foreground surface.
- Avoid abrupt changes in glass tint.
- Restore the underlying screen correctly when dismissed.

Use a predictable reverse transition when closing an overlay.

For bottom sheets, preserve drag-to-dismiss behavior and allow the user to dismiss them immediately without waiting for an entrance animation to finish.

