# PHASE 12 — ACCESSIBILITY AND REDUCED MOTION

## 34. Respect System Preferences

Inspect the current Compose APIs and Android version targets for animation-scale and reduced-motion support.

Where feasible:

- Respect system animation scales.
- Offer simplified transitions when reduced motion is enabled.
- Do not require animations to reveal essential content.
- Ensure haptics are not required to understand an action.
- Keep screen-reader focus stable through transitions.
- Preserve keyboard and D-pad navigation.
- Ensure selected, disabled, error, and success states are exposed semantically.

Do not invent a global reduced-motion preference API if it is not available in the current project. Use supported mechanisms and document any limitations.

## 35. Avoid Accessibility Regressions

Do not:

- Remove accessible labels while replacing icons.
- Make the visual dock indicator the only indication of the selected destination.
- Communicate errors through color alone.
- Use motion that causes repeated accessibility announcements.
- Delay focus movement until a long animation completes.
- Make controls unreachable during animated layout changes.

---

# PHASE 13 — PERFORMANCE AND STABILITY

## 36. Low-Memory Device Considerations

The application must remain responsive on low-memory Android hardware.

- Prefer hardware-accelerated, inexpensive transforms when appropriate.
- Avoid continuously animating large blurred surfaces.
- Avoid recomposing large document trees for local press feedback.
- Avoid allocating animation state inside frequently recomposed loops.
- Use stable keys for animated list items.
- Avoid animated content-size changes on enormous grids.
- Do not animate every spreadsheet cell during scrolling.
- Keep PDF rendering and annotation operations responsive.
- Do not execute file I/O or expensive computations on the main thread.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Do not upgrade the project's minimum SDK.

Animation must not cause document corruption, cursor loss, repeated writes, duplicated recent files, or navigation errors.

## 37. State and Lifecycle Correctness

Audit animation behavior during:

- Rapid repeated taps.
- Rotation or configuration changes where supported.
- App backgrounding and resuming.
- Keyboard opening and closing.
- Navigation during an active animation.
- Dialog dismissal during an animation.
- Save completion while navigating away.
- Failed network or file operations, where applicable.

Use stable state ownership and lifecycle-aware collection for relevant asynchronous state.

Avoid stale callbacks, duplicated collectors, and effects that run more than once unintentionally.

